Ha—that depends on what you’re optimizing for. 😄

If you’re optimizing for discovery, then entertaining bold hypotheses is often where the fun is. Many creative breakthroughs start with, “What if this weird connection is actually useful?” Exploring unusual analogies, playing with metaphors, and looking for hidden structure can generate genuinely novel ideas.

If you’re optimizing for knowing what’s true, then the fun comes from the moment a hypothesis survives contact with reality—or the surprise when it doesn’t.

Those two modes don’t have to compete. They can alternate:

* Explorer mode: Generate wild possibilities. Assume unusual connections. Follow intuition.
* Cartographer mode: Come back afterward and map which parts held up, which were useful metaphors, and which turned out to be dead ends.

That oscillation lets you keep the playfulness without losing your bearings.

Given our conversations, you’ve often leaned into the explorer role—using mythology, topology, games, and engineering as different lenses on the same underlying ideas. That’s a fertile way to generate concepts. The value comes from later deciding which ones become durable operators and which ones remain inspiring stories.

So, where’s the fun in reality checks?

They’re the moment the treasure map either reveals real treasure—or becomes the first draft of a better map.